lesson "Conjugate and Modulus" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Complex Numbers"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "Ruler"
  summary: "Two tools that turn complex numbers back into real ones. The conjugate that cancels the imaginary part, and the modulus that measures size."
  skills: ["cplx-conjugate", "cplx-modulus", "cplx-arithmetic"]

  slide "The Conjugate" {
    id: "cx3-conjugate"
    cat: "The Mirror Number"
    > The **conjugate** of $z = a + bi$ is $\bar{z} = a - bi$. Flip the sign of the
    > imaginary part and leave everything else alone.
    >
    > Two products make it valuable, and both come out real:
    >
    > $$z + \bar{z} = 2a \qquad z\bar{z} = a^2 + b^2$$
    >
    > Adding gives twice the real part; multiplying gives the sum of the squares. In
    > both cases the imaginary parts destroy each other, which is exactly what you
    > want a conjugate for.
    >
    > There is a third fact worth knowing: complex roots of real quadratics
    > **always come in conjugate pairs**. If $2 + 3i$ is a root, $2 - 3i$ is too.
    > That is why the imaginary parts vanish when you multiply the factors back
    > together, leaving the real coefficients you started with.

    numeric {
      ask "If $z = 6 - 5i$, what is $z + \bar{z}$?"
      skill: "cplx-conjugate"
      answer: 12
      expect: 2*6
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$\bar{z} = 6 + 5i$. Add them and see what survives."
      ! "$(6 - 5i) + (6 + 5i) = 12$, which is twice the real part. The imaginary parts cancel exactly, every time."
    }
  }

  slide "How Big Is a Complex Number?" {
    id: "cx3-modulus"
    cat: "Measuring Size"
    > You cannot ask whether $3 + 4i$ is bigger than $5i$ — complex numbers do not
    > sit in an order the way real numbers do. There is no "greater than" here.
    >
    > But you can ask how **far from zero** each one is, and that is the **modulus**:
    >
    > $$|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$$
    >
    > which is Pythagoras again, exactly as in the coordinate geometry unit. Plot
    > $a + bi$ at the point $(a, b)$ and the modulus is its distance from the origin.
    >
    > Notice $|z|^2 = a^2 + b^2 = z\bar{z}$. The conjugate product and the modulus are
    > the same quantity, which is why the conjugate keeps turning up.
    >
    > Drag the point and watch the distance.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6, 6]
      y: [-5, 5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param a = 3 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      param b = 4 { range: [-4, 4], step: 1 }
      vector z = (0, 0) -> (a, b) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      line across = (0, 0) -> (a, 0) { color: warning, width: 3, style: dashed }
      line up = (a, 0) -> (a, b) { color: success, width: 3, style: dashed }
      point P = (a, b) { drag: xy -> (a, b), color: accent, r: 9, snap: 1 }
      point conj = (a, 0 - b) { color: neutral, r: 7 }
      label at (-5.7, 4.5) = "z = ${a} + ${b}i" { color: primary, show: b >= 0 }
      label at (-5.7, 4.5) = "z = ${a} - ${0 - b}i" { color: primary, show: b < 0 }
      label at (-5.7, 3.6) = "modulus = ${hypot(a, b)}" { color: accent }
      label at (-5.7, 2.7) = "the grey point is the conjugate" { color: neutral }
    }

    goal "Find a z whose modulus is exactly 5" {
      when: abs(hypot(a, b) - 5) < 0.01
      hint: "3 and 4, in either order, or either sign."
    }
  }

  slide "The Conjugate Is a Reflection" {
    id: "cx3-reflection"
    cat: "Measuring Size"
    > On that diagram the conjugate is easy to spot: it is the **mirror image in the
    > real axis**. Negating the imaginary part flips the point vertically and does
    > nothing else.
    >
    > Which immediately tells you something: $z$ and $\bar{z}$ are the same distance
    > from the origin, so
    >
    > $$|\bar{z}| = |z|$$
    >
    > Reflecting cannot change how far away something is.

    quiz {
      ask "If $|z| = 13$, what is $|\bar{z}|$?"
      skill: "cplx-modulus"
      * "$13$"
      - "$-13$" { why: "A modulus is a distance, so it is never negative. Conjugating flips the point across the axis, but distance does not care about direction." }
      - "$\dfrac{1}{13}$" { why: "That would be the reciprocal, which is a different operation entirely." }
      hint "The conjugate is a reflection. Does reflecting move a point closer to the origin?"
      ! "$13$. The point moves, but not its distance from zero."
    }
  }

  slide "Compute a Modulus" {
    id: "cx3-compute"
    cat: "Measuring Size"
    numeric {
      ask "What is $|5 - 12i|$?"
      skill: "cplx-modulus"
      answer: 13
      expect: hypot(5, 12)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$\sqrt{5^2 + 12^2}$. The minus sign disappears when you square."
      onwrong: "cx3-signs-vanish" retry
      ! "$\sqrt{25 + 144} = 13$. The $5, 12, 13$ triangle again."
    }
  }

  slide "Squaring Erases the Signs" {
    id: "cx3-signs-vanish"
    hidden: true
    cat: "Measuring Size"
    > A modulus can never be negative, and the formula guarantees it: both parts get
    > **squared** before anything is added, so any minus signs disappear before they
    > can do harm.
    >
    > That means all four of these have the same modulus of $13$:
    >
    > $$5 + 12i \qquad 5 - 12i \qquad -5 + 12i \qquad -5 - 12i$$
    >
    > They sit in four different quadrants, mirror images of each other, all exactly
    > $13$ from the origin. If you ever produce a negative modulus, you have
    > subtracted where you should have squared.

    scene plane {
      x: [-8, 8]
      y: [-8, 8]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      circle ring = (0, 0) { r: 6.5, color: neutral, opacity: 0 }
      point p1 = (2.5, 6) { color: primary, r: 8 }
      point p2 = (2.5, -6) { color: primary, r: 8 }
      point p3 = (-2.5, 6) { color: primary, r: 8 }
      point p4 = (-2.5, -6) { color: primary, r: 8 }
      vector v1 = (0, 0) -> (2.5, 6) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      vector v2 = (0, 0) -> (2.5, -6) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      vector v3 = (0, 0) -> (-2.5, 6) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      vector v4 = (0, 0) -> (-2.5, -6) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      label at (0, 7.4) = "all four sit on the same circle" { anchor: middle, color: neutral }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "What is $|-8 + 6i|$?"
      skill: "cplx-modulus"
      answer: 10
      expect: hypot(0 - 8, 6)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$\sqrt{(-8)^2 + 6^2}$, and $(-8)^2$ is $+64$."
      ! "$\sqrt{64 + 36} = 10$. The negative real part changes where the point sits, never how far away it is."
    }
  }

  slide "Moduli Multiply" {
    id: "cx3-multiply"
    cat: "A Useful Property"
    > One property that saves real effort:
    >
    > $$|z_1 z_2| = |z_1| \times |z_2|$$
    >
    > The modulus of a product is the product of the moduli. So to find how big
    > $(3+4i)(5+12i)$ is, you never have to multiply them out: $5 \times 13 = 65$.
    >
    > Note that the same is emphatically **not** true for addition. $|z_1 + z_2|$ is
    > generally not $|z_1| + |z_2|$, for exactly the reason two vectors of length $3$
    > and $4$ do not have to add to $7$. It depends which way they point.

    numeric {
      ask "Given $|z_1| = 5$ and $|z_2| = 13$, what is $|z_1 z_2|$?"
      skill: "cplx-modulus"
      answer: 65
      expect: 5*13
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Multiply the moduli."
      ! "$65$. Expanding the product first would have given the same answer after a lot more work."
    }
  }

  slide "Putting Them Together" {
    id: "cx3-synth"
    cat: "A Useful Property"
    numeric {
      ask "For $z = 3 + 4i$, what is $z\bar{z}$?"
      skill: "cplx-conjugate"
      answer: 25
      expect: 3^2 + 4^2
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$z\bar{z} = a^2 + b^2$, which is also $|z|^2$."
      ! "$25$, which is $|z|^2 = 5^2$. The three ideas are one idea: $z\bar{z}$, $a^2+b^2$ and $|z|^2$ are all the same number."
    }
  }
}
